1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,704
Aarav connects a small microphone
straight to a big speaker.

2
00:00:04,004 --> 00:00:05,683
His voice almost vanishes.

3
00:00:05,683 --> 00:00:08,872
The heavy speaker pulls
too much from the weak mic.

4
00:00:09,172 --> 00:00:14,085
He adds one transistor stage in between,
with a voltage gain of only one.

5
00:00:14,385 --> 00:00:16,557
Now his voice comes through clearly.

6
00:00:16,557 --> 00:00:18,170
Meet the emitter follower.

7
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:21,303
Think of a strong helper
who copies your every move.

8
00:00:21,653 --> 00:00:23,502
You lift your hand a little.

9
00:00:23,502 --> 00:00:26,299
The helper lifts a heavy
box the same amount.

10
00:00:26,649 --> 00:00:28,401
You use almost no effort.

11
00:00:28,401 --> 00:00:30,543
The helper supplies the strength.

12
00:00:30,893 --> 00:00:32,515
The movement is the same.

13
00:00:32,515 --> 00:00:34,372
But the power is much bigger.

14
00:00:35,022 --> 00:00:37,130
An emitter follower is that helper.

15
00:00:37,130 --> 00:00:38,872
Its output copies its input.

16
00:00:39,522 --> 00:00:41,440
Let's build it on the breadboard.

17
00:00:41,790 --> 00:00:43,989
The collector goes straight to the supply.

18
00:00:44,339 --> 00:00:48,655
The output is taken from the
emitter, across the resistor RE.

19
00:00:49,005 --> 00:00:53,659
So it is called a common collector
amplifier, or an emitter follower.

20
00:00:54,009 --> 00:00:56,449
Now we feed a slow wave into the base.

21
00:00:56,799 --> 00:00:59,575
The emitter rises and falls with the base.

22
00:00:59,575 --> 00:01:00,684
It follows it.

23
00:01:01,034 --> 00:01:03,830
It stays about zero
point seven volts below.

24
00:01:03,830 --> 00:01:05,844
That is the base emitter drop.

25
00:01:06,194 --> 00:01:09,135
On the scope, the input
and output move together.

26
00:01:09,135 --> 00:01:10,359
They are in phase.

27
00:01:10,709 --> 00:01:13,573
The output is almost as big as the input.

28
00:01:13,573 --> 00:01:15,700
The voltage gain is about one.

29
00:01:16,050 --> 00:01:17,631
But look at the currents.

30
00:01:17,631 --> 00:01:20,564
A tiny base current controls
a big emitter current.

31
00:01:20,914 --> 00:01:22,865
So the current gain is large.

32
00:01:22,865 --> 00:01:24,673
It is about beta plus one.

33
00:01:25,323 --> 00:01:27,587
Why is the voltage gain about one?

34
00:01:27,587 --> 00:01:28,867
Look at the loop.

35
00:01:29,217 --> 00:01:34,240
The input voltage is the base emitter
voltage, plus the output voltage.

36
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:39,030
So the whole output is fed
back, in series with the input.

37
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:40,297
Beta equals one.

38
00:01:40,997 --> 00:01:43,670
This is voltage series negative feedback.

39
00:01:43,670 --> 00:01:45,529
One hundred percent of it.

40
00:01:46,179 --> 00:01:50,243
With beta equal to one, A f
equals A, over one plus A.

41
00:01:50,943 --> 00:01:53,822
A is large, so A f is just under one.

42
00:01:53,822 --> 00:01:55,087
Never above one.

43
00:01:55,787 --> 00:02:00,668
More exactly, the voltage gain
is RE, over small r e plus RE.

44
00:02:01,368 --> 00:02:04,631
Small r e is the tiny
resistance inside the emitter.

45
00:02:04,631 --> 00:02:07,458
It is about twenty five
millivolts over IE.

46
00:02:08,108 --> 00:02:10,897
The same voltage out,
with far more current.

47
00:02:10,897 --> 00:02:12,130
That is a buffer.

48
00:02:12,930 --> 00:02:15,233
Its real superpower is impedance.

49
00:02:15,583 --> 00:02:19,470
Input impedance tells how much
a circuit pulls from its source.

50
00:02:19,470 --> 00:02:20,744
High means gentle.

51
00:02:21,094 --> 00:02:24,612
An emitter follower has a
very high input impedance.

52
00:02:24,612 --> 00:02:26,273
About beta times RE.

53
00:02:26,623 --> 00:02:28,746
And a very low output impedance.

54
00:02:28,746 --> 00:02:30,783
So it can drive a heavy load.

55
00:02:31,133 --> 00:02:34,192
So it sits between a weak
source and a heavy load.

56
00:02:34,192 --> 00:02:35,583
We call it a buffer.

57
00:02:36,233 --> 00:02:38,577
This job is called impedance matching.

58
00:02:39,227 --> 00:02:40,280
Worked example.

59
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:44,060
A weak source gives one volt,
with ten kilo ohms inside it.

60
00:02:44,410 --> 00:02:46,840
It must drive a load of one kilo ohm.

61
00:02:47,190 --> 00:02:51,428
Connected directly, the load gets
one volt times one over eleven.

62
00:02:51,428 --> 00:02:53,627
Just zero point zero nine volts.

63
00:02:54,327 --> 00:02:57,878
Now add an emitter follower,
with beta one hundred.

64
00:02:57,878 --> 00:02:59,539
The load is its RE.

65
00:02:59,889 --> 00:03:03,110
Its input impedance is
about beta times RE.

66
00:03:03,110 --> 00:03:05,383
That is one hundred kilo ohms.

67
00:03:06,083 --> 00:03:09,821
The source now keeps one hundred
over one hundred and ten.

68
00:03:09,821 --> 00:03:11,989
About zero point nine one volts.

69
00:03:12,689 --> 00:03:15,926
The follower passes this on
with a gain of about one.

70
00:03:15,926 --> 00:03:18,690
So the load gets about
zero point nine volts.

71
00:03:19,390 --> 00:03:22,989
Ten times more voltage, from
a stage with a gain of one.

72
00:03:23,639 --> 00:03:25,103
Now, the exam trap.

73
00:03:25,453 --> 00:03:29,675
Many students think the emitter follower
flips the signal, like the common

74
00:03:29,675 --> 00:03:30,849
emitter amplifier.

75
00:03:31,199 --> 00:03:32,099
Wrong.

76
00:03:31,743 --> 00:03:35,980
The output is in phase with the
input, and the gain is just under one.

77
00:03:36,630 --> 00:03:38,895
And a gain of one is not useless.

78
00:03:38,895 --> 00:03:41,589
The current gain and power gain are large.

79
00:03:41,939 --> 00:03:43,560
Where is this asked?

80
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:45,420
In PUC board papers.

81
00:03:46,070 --> 00:03:50,603
Draw the circuit, explain why the
gain is about one, and compare it with

82
00:03:50,603 --> 00:03:51,714
CE and CB.

83
00:03:52,364 --> 00:03:53,264
Try this.

84
00:03:53,219 --> 00:03:58,014
An emitter follower has beta one
hundred and fifty, and RE of two kilo

85
00:03:58,014 --> 00:03:58,914
ohms.

86
00:03:58,598 --> 00:04:02,392
What are its input impedance
and voltage gain, roughly?

87
00:04:07,792 --> 00:04:08,692
A.

88
00:04:08,202 --> 00:04:12,675
About three hundred kilo ohms,
and a gain of about one, in phase.

89
00:04:13,025 --> 00:04:16,120
The input impedance is
about beta times RE.

90
00:04:16,470 --> 00:04:21,330
One hundred and fifty times two kilo
ohms is three hundred kilo ohms.

91
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:23,709
The output follows the input.

92
00:04:23,709 --> 00:04:27,372
So the gain is just under
one, with no phase change.

93
00:04:28,022 --> 00:04:29,568
Screenshot this card.

94
00:04:32,768 --> 00:04:34,386
That is all for now.

95
00:04:34,386 --> 00:04:38,738
More PUC Electronics is coming
soon, so follow the playlist.
